    <article class="docs-prose">
      <h1 id="custom-builds-buildah" class="group scroll-mt-20">Custom image builds with Buildah<a href="#custom-builds-buildah" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p>With OpenShift Container Platform 4.22, a docker socket will not be present on the host nodes. This means the <em>mount docker socket</em> option of a custom build is not guaranteed to provide an accessible docker socket for use within a custom build image.</p>
      <p>If you require this capability in order to build and push images, add the Buildah tool your custom build image and use it to build and push the image within your custom build logic. The following is an example of how to run custom builds with Buildah.</p>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Using the custom build strategy requires permissions that normal users do not have by default because it allows the user to execute arbitrary code inside a privileged container running on the cluster. This level of access can be used to compromise the cluster and therefore should be granted only to users who are trusted with administrative privileges on the cluster.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <h2 id="_prerequisites" class="group scroll-mt-20">Prerequisites<a href="#_prerequisites" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <ul><li>Review how to <a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/cicd/builds/securing-builds-by-strategy#securing-builds-by-strategy">grant custom build permissions</a>.</li></ul>
      <h2 id="builds-create-custom-build-artifacts_custom-builds-buildah" class="group scroll-mt-20">Creating custom build artifacts<a href="#builds-create-custom-build-artifacts_custom-builds-buildah" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>You must create the image you want to use as your custom build image.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Starting with an empty directory, create a file named <code>Dockerfile</code> with the following content:
      <CodeBlock code={"FROM registry.redhat.io/rhel8/buildah\n# In this example, `/tmp/build` contains the inputs that build when this\n# custom builder image is run. Normally the custom builder image fetches\n# this content from some location at build time, by using git clone as an example.\nADD dockerfile.sample /tmp/input/Dockerfile\nADD build.sh /usr/bin\nRUN chmod a+x /usr/bin/build.sh\n# /usr/bin/build.sh contains the actual custom build logic that will be run when\n# this custom builder image is run.\nENTRYPOINT [\"/usr/bin/build.sh\"]"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      In the same directory, create a file named <code>dockerfile.sample</code>. This file is included in the custom build image and defines the image that is produced by the custom build:
      <CodeBlock code={"FROM registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi\nRUN touch /tmp/build"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      In the same directory, create a file named <code>build.sh</code>. This file contains the logic that is run when the custom build runs:
      <CodeBlock code={"#!/bin/sh\n# Note that in this case the build inputs are part of the custom builder image, but normally this\n# is retrieved from an external source.\ncd /tmp/input\n# OUTPUT_REGISTRY and OUTPUT_IMAGE are env variables provided by the custom\n# build framework\nTAG=\"${OUTPUT_REGISTRY}/${OUTPUT_IMAGE}\"\n\n\n# performs the build of the new image defined by dockerfile.sample\nbuildah --storage-driver vfs bud --isolation chroot -t ${TAG} .\n\n\n# buildah requires a slight modification to the push secret provided by the service\n# account to use it for pushing the image\ncp /var/run/secrets/openshift.io/push/.dockercfg /tmp\n(echo \"{ \\\"auths\\\": \" ; cat /var/run/secrets/openshift.io/push/.dockercfg ; echo \"}\") > /tmp/.dockercfg\n\n\n# push the new image to the target for the build\nbuildah --storage-driver vfs push --tls-verify=false --authfile /tmp/.dockercfg ${TAG}"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="builds-build-custom-builder-image_custom-builds-buildah" class="group scroll-mt-20">Build custom builder image<a href="#builds-build-custom-builder-image_custom-builds-buildah" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>You can use OpenShift Container Platform to build and push custom builder images to use in a custom strategy.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul><li>Define all the inputs that will go into creating your new custom builder image.</li></ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Define a <code>BuildConfig</code> object that will build your custom builder image:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc new-build --binary --strategy=docker --name custom-builder-image"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      From the directory in which you created your custom build image, run the build:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc start-build custom-builder-image --from-dir . -F"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>After the build completes, your new custom builder image is available in your project in an image stream tag that is named <code>custom-builder-image:latest</code>.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="builds-use-custom-builder-image_custom-builds-buildah" class="group scroll-mt-20">Use custom builder image<a href="#builds-use-custom-builder-image_custom-builds-buildah" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>You can define a <code>BuildConfig</code> object that uses the custom strategy in conjunction with your custom builder image to execute your custom build logic.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>Define all the required inputs for new custom builder image.</li>
      <li>Build your custom builder image.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a file named <code>buildconfig.yaml</code>. This file defines the <code>BuildConfig</code> object that is created in your project and executed:
      <CodeBlock code={"kind: BuildConfig\napiVersion: build.openshift.io/v1\nmetadata:\n  name: sample-custom-build\n  labels:\n    name: sample-custom-build\n  annotations:\n    template.alpha.openshift.io/wait-for-ready: 'true'\nspec:\n  strategy:\n    type: Custom\n    customStrategy:\n      forcePull: true\n      from:\n        kind: ImageStreamTag\n        name: custom-builder-image:latest\n        namespace: <yourproject> (1)\n  output:\n    to:\n      kind: ImageStreamTag\n      name: sample-custom:latest"} lang="yaml" />
      <ol><li>Specify your project name.</li></ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>BuildConfig</code> object by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f buildconfig.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a file named <code>imagestream.yaml</code>. This file defines the image stream to which the build will push the image:
      <CodeBlock code={"kind: ImageStream\napiVersion: image.openshift.io/v1\nmetadata:\n  name: sample-custom\nspec: {}"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the image stream by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f imagestream.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Run your custom build by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc start-build sample-custom-build -F"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>When the build runs, it launches a pod running the custom builder image that was built earlier. The pod runs the <code>build.sh</code> logic that is defined as the entrypoint for the custom builder image. The <code>build.sh</code> logic invokes Buildah to build the <code>dockerfile.sample</code> that was embedded in the custom builder image, and then uses Buildah to push the new image to the <code>sample-custom image stream</code>.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
